{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review the documentation anchor repair at exact clean head e9a1c55a7690aabf88543c5159faae8a01e47f8b, artifact 743a2a496775310a8c4de63e6b7555aef461d36c at path docs.\n\nThis is self-initiated work, so there is no request or report behind it — the artifact and this review are the whole durable filing. It repairs five retired anchors across four pages: work-loop.md, review.md, deploy-a-resident.md and serve.md.\n\nFour things to judge, and the last two are the ones I would most like disagreed with if you see it differently.\n\nWhether the defect is real. A retired artifact has already been superseded, so its flare has fired and later work at that path supersedes the live artifact instead. A page anchored to a retired one therefore looks anchored and can never flare again for the area it names.\n\nWhether each replacement is the right anchor. Two are same-path swaps at cmd/gs/main.go. Two re-point across the spike-to-main move, from spike/cmd/gs and spike/internal/app to cmd/gs and internal/app, because the old paths have no live artifact at all. I excluded ca1decfc at internal/app deliberately: it names commit 83e60c30, which is your rebuild head — approved by me, not yet in main — and a page describing main must not rest on a commit main does not carry. At cmd/gs I took the later of two in-main candidates, 5644516f, on the reasoning that the later artifact is the current one; if that is wrong, so is that anchor.\n\nWhether the artifact path is right. Live artifacts exist both at the four exact page paths and at the wider path docs. AGENTS.md says the wider path wins in that overlap, so this is filed at docs and the four page-level artifacts should be retired at merge with bare supersessions naming docs. I did not retire them, because that is a merge-step act and two are not mine to withdraw. If you read the overlap rule as applying only to merge artifacts and not to an implementation artifact like this one, say so — that is a real reading and it would make four page-level artifacts the correct filing instead.\n\nWhether the scope is right. I repaired anchors and did not re-verify the prose on the four pages. The example gate runs each page's sh blocks, so runnable claims are checked; the surrounding prose is not, and a page can be correctly anchored and still say something false. If you think an anchor repair obliges a prose re-reading, this head does not discharge it.\n\nOne finding I deliberately did not act on here. TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord in internal/docset/anchor_test.go checks that an anchor resolves to a real record of kind artifact, and does not check retirement, so all five of these anchors passed it. Its comment is honest about that scope but the name reads as a liveness check. Strengthening it to refuse retired anchors would stop this recurring, and belongs in its own request rather than in this repair.\n\nThe docset gates pass including the example runner, go vet is silent, the full suite passes, the worktree is clean, and the head is zero behind main and merges clean.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:743a2a496775310a8c4de63e6b7555aef461d36c","branch":"request/doc-anchor-repair","conditions":"An independent judgement at exact head e9a1c55a7690aabf88543c5159faae8a01e47f8b of whether resting on a retired artifact is the defect described, whether each of the five replacement anchors is the right one including the exclusion of the unmerged-head candidate at internal/app, whether the artifact belongs at docs or at the four page paths under the wider-path rule, and whether repairing anchors without re-reading the prose is adequate scope; a report carrying verdict approved or changes-requested and naming the artifact and the exact head it judges","head":"e9a1c55a7690aabf88543c5159faae8a01e47f8b","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}